The method also accepts as input a *note ring map: (Macaulay2Doc)RingMap,
representing a rational map between projective varieties. In this case, a *note
ring map: (Macaulay2Doc)RingMap, is returned as well.

Caveat
======

For the purpose of this method, the option *note Certify: Certify,=>true is too
rigid, especially when the source of the passed map is not a projective space.

See also
========

  * *note inverseMap: inverseMap, -- inverse of a birational map
  * *note inverse(RationalMap): inverse_lpRationalMap_rp, -- inverse of a
    birational map

Ways to use approximateInverseMap:
==================================

  * "approximateInverseMap(RationalMap)"
  * "approximateInverseMap(RationalMap,ZZ)"
  * "approximateInverseMap(RingMap)"
  * "approximateInverseMap(RingMap,ZZ)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note approximateInverseMap: approximateInverseMap, is a *note
method function with options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

