inverseMap -- inverse of a birational map
*****************************************

  * Usage: 
        inverseMap phi
  * Inputs:
      * phi, a *note rational map: RationalMap,, a birational map
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note BlowUpStrategy: BlowUpStrategy, => ..., default value
        "Eliminate",
      * *note Certify: Certify, => ..., default value false, whether to ensure
        correctness of output
      * *note Verbose: inverseMap_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVerbose=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value true,
  * Outputs:
      * a *note rational map: RationalMap,, the inverse map of phi

Description
===========

If the source variety is a projective space and if a further technical
condition is satisfied, then the algorithm used is that described in the paper
by Russo and Simis - On birational maps and Jacobian matrices - Compos. Math.
126 (3), 335-358, 2001. For the general case, the algorithm used is the same as
for *note invertBirationalMap: (Parametrization)invertBirationalMap, in the
package *note Parametrization: (Parametrization)Top,. Note that in this case,
the analogous method *note inverseOfMap: (RationalMaps)inverseOfMap, in the
package *note RationalMaps: (RationalMaps)Top, generally turns out to be
faster.

