rationalMap(Ideal,ZZ,ZZ) -- makes a rational map from an ideal
**************************************************************

  * Function: *note rationalMap: rationalMap,
  * Usage: 
        rationalMap(I,d,e)
        rationalMap(I,d)
        rationalMap I
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, a homogeneous ideal in the
        coordinate ring of a projective variety $X\subseteq\mathbb{P}^n$
      * d, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, a positive integer (or a *note
        list: (Macaulay2Doc)List, of integers in the case $X$ is embedded in a
        product of projective spaces)
      * e, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, a positive integer (if omitted,
        it is taken to be 1)
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Dominant: Dominant, => ..., default value null, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note rational map: RationalMap,, the rational map $X\dashrightarrow
        \mathbb{P}^m$ defined by the linear system of hypersurfaces of degree
        $d$ having points of multiplicity $e$ along the projective subscheme of
        $X$ defined by $I$.

Description
===========

In most cases, the command rationalMap(I,d,e) yields the same output as
rationalMap(saturate(I^e),d), but the former is implemented using pure linear
algebra.

The command rationalMap I is basically equivalent to rationalMap(I,max degrees
I).

In the following example, we calculate the rational map defined by the linear
system of cubic hypersurfaces in $\mathbb{P}^6$ having double points along a
Veronese surface $V\subset\mathbb{P}^5\subset\mathbb{P}^6$.

